{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nA completion-gated close over MCP committed `STATUS in_progress -> closed` (with `completion_expectation: \"required\"`) and then wrote no `completion-verifier` attestation. The close was not forced, but a closed, required, uncertified ticket looks exactly like a forced close. Root cause is confirmed (evidence in the RCA comment). The MCP container was retired by autodeploy mid-close:\n\n1. `_mcp_health.CERTIFIED_TOOLS` (review_plan, verify_completion, review_code, scan_spec) is the only set the SIGTERM drain waits for. `transition_ticket` is not in it, so the drain saw 0 in flight and set `should_exit` at once, and uvicorn's 30 s backstop cut the request (\"Transport closed\").\n2. The abandoned AnyIO worker kept running the close.\n3. `run_mcp` returned, and `mcp_server.main()`'s `finally: binding.cleanup()` deleted the box signer's key copy.\n4. The non-bundled close path (`completion_bundle._publish_close` -> `transition_close._sign_completion_and_report`) committed STATUS, then signing raised \"the bound op-cert key ... does not exist\" and degraded to `cause=sign_failed`.\n\nThe same race applies to the background gate daemons (`verify_completion_start` / `review_plan_start` / `run_workflow`, all spawned through `_opcert_binding.spawn_context_daemon`). They sign with the same binding, and neither the drain nor the cleanup waits for them.\n\nPrior intent: story b03c-f8d4-ef82-4618 says enabling the atomic close bundle (`verify.completion_pinned_ticket_view`) by default is a separate, evidence-based decision, so it is out of scope here. The drain's documented purpose (ADR 0104, `_mcp_health` docstring) is that signing work in flight is never cut off by retirement. This fix restores that purpose.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. `src/rebar/_mcp_health.py`\n   - Add `SIGNING_TOOLS = {\"transition_ticket\", \"sign_manifest\", \"sign_review\"}`: the non-LLM tools whose body can sign with the bound key. `transition_ticket` closes with a completion certificate, `sign_manifest` can sign any op-cert kind, and `sign_review` signs plan-review.\n   - The gauge tracks and drains `DRAINED_TOOLS = CERTIFIED_TOOLS | SIGNING_TOOLS`. `CERTIFIED_TOOLS` keeps its meaning (billable LLM tools).\n   - Add `InFlightGauge.hold()`, which counts accepted background work and returns an idempotent release.\n   - Add a context-local serving gauge (`serving_gauge(gauge)` context manager plus `hold_background_work()`), so work spawned from a request is counted against the server that accepted it.\n2. `src/rebar/_opcert_binding.py`: `spawn_context_daemon` takes the hold in the spawning thread, before `start()`, and releases it in the daemon's `finally`. A tool call therefore never returns to 0 before its daemon is counted. This is the single spawn chokepoint that the ff4a structural test already enforces.\n3. `src/rebar/_mcp_serving.py`\n   - Bind the gauge with the signer inside the HTTP serving thread and around stdio `server.run`.\n   - Add `wait_for_idle(gauge)`, which `run_mcp` runs in a `finally` before returning, so `mcp_server.main()`'s `binding.cleanup()` happens only after in-flight work ends. There is no new deadline: the tracked work is already bounded by its own caps (the gate hard cap), and Docker's `stop --time` stays the outer bound.\n4. fsck: `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_scan.py`, check 2 (`_check_create_events`), already reduces every ticket. It now emits `UNCERTIFIED_CLOSE: <id> — closed with completion_expectation=required but no completion-verifier attestation; ...` for closed tickets with `completion_expectation == \"required\"` and no `completion-verifier` key in `attestations`.\n   - The detail names the remedy: run `verify_completion_start` / `rebar verify-completion` on the closed ticket, which signs without reopening, as was done for `6b66-9378-ebb1-446c`.\n   - Report-only (`counted: false`), like `STATUS_FORK_RESOLVED`: historical findings must not pin every store's fsck at exit 1, and this ticket does not re-sign them.\n   - `src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py` adds the kind to `_STRUCTURED_KINDS` and `_NEVER_COUNTED_SCAN_KINDS`. `src/rebar/schemas/fsck.schema.json` lists it among the report-only kinds.\n5. Docs.\n   - `docs/plan-review-gate.md` (the `-32001` section): what is durable when the transport dies. A server-side gate or close keeps running and, with this fix, is drained before the container retires, keeping its key. The STATUS commit and the signature in the non-bundled close are still two writes, so a hard kill between them leaves an `UNCERTIFIED_CLOSE`, which fsck reports, and that names the remedy.\n   - `docs/user-guide.md`: the fsck kind.\n6. Tests (each fails on origin/main before the fix):\n   - `tests/unit/test_mcp_drain_signing_iguanodon.py`:\n     - the instrumented `transition_ticket` moves the gauge and is refused while draining;\n     - `spawn_context_daemon` under a bound serving gauge holds it until the daemon ends;\n     - `run_mcp` (stdio, stubbed server) does not return while a hold is outstanding;\n     - end to end: a box-key-bound close that reaches signing after SIGTERM produces closed AND certified, never closed-and-unsigned, because the drain keeps the key alive until it finishes.\n   - `tests/unit/test_fsck_uncertified_close.py`: flags a required close with no certificate; does not flag a signed close, a `force_bypassed` close, or a `disposition` close; `counted` is false.\n   - Update the pinned set in `tests/interfaces/facades/test_fsck_issue_count_29c3.py`.\n   - `tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp_http_transport.py::test_gauge_begin_draining_refuses_new_certified_ops_2f46` uses `sign_review` as its example of an undrained tool. Switch it to `list_tickets`, since `sign_review` is now drained.\n\nClaim-gate audit (AC4): `claim_ticket` mints no certificate. It checks an existing plan-review attestation. `review_plan` (sync) was already drained, and `review_plan_start` / `sign_review` are covered by steps 1 and 2.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Root cause identified with evidence distinguishing the 3 original hypotheses (RCA comment).\n- [ ] The SIGTERM drain waits for in-flight `transition_ticket`, `sign_manifest`, `sign_review`, and for every daemon spawned through `spawn_context_daemon`; a regression test fails on origin/main and passes after.\n- [ ] `run_mcp` returns, and so `binding.cleanup()` runs, only after the in-flight gauge reaches 0; regression test.\n- [ ] Regression test: a completion-gated close that reaches signing after SIGTERM ends closed WITH a certified completion attestation, never closed-and-unsigned.\n- [ ] Claim gate audited: `claim_ticket` signs nothing; the plan-review signing paths share the drain (recorded in a comment).\n- [ ] `rebar store fsck` reports `UNCERTIFIED_CLOSE` (report-only, `counted: false`) for closed `completion_expectation=required` tickets with no completion-verifier attestation; tests cover the signed, forced, and disposition negatives. Against the live store it reports exactly the 3 known tickets (damageable-huge-xiphosuran, cutprice-untameable-stork, trifling-countable-armadillo), which are NOT re-signed or modified.\n- [ ] `docs/plan-review-gate.md` states what is and is not durable when the MCP transport dies; `docs/user-guide.md` documents the new fsck kind.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass; targeted tests pass; Gerrit `LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1`.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Enabling `verify.completion_pinned_ticket_view` (atomic close bundle) by default or on the box (b03c: separate decision).\n- Re-signing or editing the 3 historical tickets.\n- The leaked signer key copies on the operator host (balladic-fattening-tortoise)."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793507823711203001,"uuid":"f0461859-c6ef-4424-914f-040b2f602414"}